language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ fancy_dress: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ Mr. Greenfan wants to give a dress party where the male guests must wear green dresses. The rules: 1. If someone wears a green tie he has to wear a green shirt. 2. A guest who wears green socks or a green shirt must also wear a green tie or a green hat. 3. A guest wearing a green shirt or a green hat or who does not wear green socks must wear a green tie. 4. A guest who is not dressed according to rules 1-3 must pay a $11 entrance fee. Mr Greenguest wants to participate but owns only a green shirt (otherwise he would have to pay $9 for one). He could buy a green tie for $10, a green hat (used) for $2 and green socks for $12. What is the cheapest way for Mr Greenguest to participate? Choose whether he wears a green tie (t), a green hat (h), a green shirt (r) and green socks (s), and whether he pays the entrance fee (n), so that the total cost is minimised.
$
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/fancy_dress.html
$ Save this model as model.eprime and run it with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the solution is
$ written to `model.eprime.solution`:
$   savilerow model.eprime -run-solver

$ t: he wears a green tie
find t : bool
$ h: he wears a green hat
find h : bool
$ r: he wears a green shirt (he owns one, so it costs nothing)
find r : bool
$ s: he wears green socks
find s : bool
$ n: he pays the entrance fee
find n : bool

minimising 10 * toInt(t) + 2 * toInt(h) + 12 * toInt(s) + 11 * toInt(n)
such that
$ rule 1: if he wears a green tie he has to wear a green shirt
(t -> r),
$ rule 2: a guest who wears green socks or a green shirt must also wear a green tie or a green hat
((s \/ r) -> (t \/ h)),
$ rule 3: a guest wearing a green shirt or a green hat or who does not wear green socks must wear a green tie
((r \/ h \/ !s) -> t),
$ rule 4: he pays the entrance fee exactly when he is not dressed according to rules 1 to 3
n <-> !((t -> r) /\ ((s \/ r) -> (t \/ h)) /\ ((r \/ h \/ !s) -> t))
